A||||12||Seanh||White Smoke||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||10-15-2000||04:10 PM||||I was wondering. When I start my car, white smoke pours from the tail pipes for a short while. Is this normal or is somthing not good? It does it all the time, hot weather, cold weather, morning, afternoons, and evening. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||10-15-2000||04:19 PM||||Hmmm...dunno to be honest. I can tell you that all of mine shoot out a puff of black smoke at first on start up...never had any probs though...not quite sure what the white smoke is all about.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||10-15-2000||04:59 PM||||i think white smoke is water. if so maybe just some condensation or water in gas or cooling system leaking into combustion chamber through head?<P>blue smoke oil?<P>black smoke too much gas in combustion mix? carbon?<P>some one help if i've mixed it up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-15-2000||05:34 PM||||If it is indeed water, tell me this. Would a bad head gasket allow water to get in the chambers? I am fixing a bad header gasket right now, and wonding if this might be the problem. It just started leaking coolant, but could have been bad for a while. It is the gasket on the front of the engine. While I am working on it, shold I replace the rear one also. I am taking the engine out of the car to fix this, among some other reasons. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000004||ThatRickGuy||10-15-2000||06:39 PM||||1 question. Head gasket or header gasket? header gasket, or exhuast manifold gasket, wouldn't have any effect as for white smoke. Head gaskets on the other had, would be the most likely culprit. White smoke is usually coolant/water in the chamber. check your oil, if it looks really high, and your coolant is low, most likely you have a bad head gasket. drain the oil out right away, coolant isn't exactly nice to the drivetrain.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||10-15-2000||08:24 PM||||Yes, I meant head gasket. It is causing my stupid coolant leak and needs replacing. I am glad I asked this question. It has been doing this for a while, but no coolant leaking. I will check my oil, but I dont think it is extremly high. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000006||Max The Chainsaw||10-15-2000||11:31 PM||||White smoke IS caused by water. If you've had a head gasket start leaking as well, that's most likely the culprit. If there is coolant in the oil, it will usually take on a brownish milky apperance before it shows as way over full. If it gets to the point where it dumps that much coolant in your oil, you will be VERY low on coolant and overheating badly.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Seanh||10-15-2000||11:39 PM||||Just a couple of weeks ago the car started leaking coolant and overheating badly. I have noticed the brownish tint to the oil, but thought nothing of it. I have not driven the car since then thank god. I am going to be replacing the gasket this week then. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000008||JetroGT||10-16-2000||06:31 AM||||You should also get the heads decked and revalved. You will notice more power. My car used to do the same thing at start up. But that was from the oil dripping past worn valves. ||||reg||14
Z||000009||Seanh||10-16-2000||07:40 AM||||Where do I get this done, or car I do it myself? Also, what will that cost me to have done. I am planning on removing them myself, but I am not sure about what you mentioned. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000010||ThatRickGuy||10-16-2000||09:42 AM||||You can replace the head gasket yourself, not sure on the cost to have someone do it. But the head rebuild you'd have to take it to a machinist. I don't have my price sheet in front of me, but to get a head decked (makes it level for a better seal) a 3 angle valve job, and new valve seals, etc... it'll probrably be arround $200-300, but after that you shouldn't have any kind of problems with the head.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||10-16-2000||05:14 PM||||I am going to start taking the engine apart once again tonight. I am going to remove the heads (I am going to do both since I have the engine apart, although I have no trouble with the rear one) and take them to NAPA to be decked and redone. What should I ask them to do specifically? I am definatly going to have them resurfaced, but what else? They said they could resurface them and inspect them and tell me what I should have done. I talked to them today and they told me it would be about $100 for the pair to be resurfaced and then additional if they need anything further. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000012||Seanh||10-16-2000||05:37 PM||||Just reading my Haynes manual and wondering, once I remove the heads, should I disassemble the heads, clean and inspect them? Why do I need to take it to NAPA? I see according to haynes manual that I can replace the seals myself. Am I just taking it to them for the valve job? If this is the only reason (besides the decking) should the head be reassembled before taking it to them? According to the haynes manual, it says the Valves are the only thing at should be serviced by the machine shop. Thanks<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
